WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:02.377
- I like to know that I can define my fame

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:04.978
and define my beauty for myself.

00:00:04.978 --> 00:00:07.800
If you are always changing what you do

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.901
based on what other people say,

00:00:09.901 --> 00:00:11.689
you completely lose yourself.

00:00:11.689 --> 00:00:14.375
Making it is important,
but it's not more important

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:16.874
than keeping plans with
your grandma and your dad.

00:00:16.874 --> 00:00:21.866
I put a lot of time and
effort into everything I do,

00:00:21.866 --> 00:00:23.775
to the point that I do nothing else.

00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:26.504
- She's an American singer,
songwriter, and actress.

00:00:26.504 --> 00:00:28.811
She's one of the best-selling
musicians of all time,

00:00:28.811 --> 00:00:31.593
with over 27 million albums sold.

00:00:31.593 --> 00:00:34.198
Her achievements include
12 Guinness World Records,

00:00:34.198 --> 00:00:35.607
and six Grammy Awards.

00:00:35.607 --> 00:00:37.612
She's Lady Gaga, and here's my take

00:00:37.612 --> 00:00:39.657
on her Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:00:39.657 --> 00:00:40.991
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:40.991 --> 00:00:42.319
and make sure to stick around all the way

00:00:42.319 --> 00:00:43.798
to the end for some special bonus clips.

00:00:43.798 --> 00:00:45.350
Also, as Lady Gaga is talking,

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.722
if she says something that is really

00:00:46.722 --> 00:00:48.118
inspiring or meaningful to you,

00:00:48.118 --> 00:00:50.415
please leave it and write it
down in the comments below

00:00:50.415 --> 00:00:52.138
and put quotes around it,
so other people can be

00:00:52.138 --> 00:00:53.811
inspired by the message as well.

00:00:53.811 --> 00:00:57.069
(jets blasting off)

00:00:57.069 --> 00:00:59.353
- I just always felt famous,

00:00:59.353 --> 00:01:00.809
that's what my first
album was really about,

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:02.490
is that fame comes from inside of you,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.763
and it's not something that you obtain.

00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:08.057
And I still feel that
way, in a lot of ways.

00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:10.558
I like to know that I can define my fame,

00:01:10.558 --> 00:01:13.209
and define my beauty for myself.

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:17.330
Which is why I'm so
vigilant and relentless

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.646
about my image and my music is

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:23.010
I will not allow, no matter
how successful I become,

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.263
for the public to define or indicate

00:01:26.263 --> 00:01:28.846
what it is that I create, or what it is

00:01:28.846 --> 00:01:30.959
that I believe is
beautiful, or what I believe

00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:32.626
is a hit pop record.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.746
That's who I am, and I
will always be that way.

00:01:37.746 --> 00:01:40.439
Relentless and fearless and vicious.

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:42.764
I'm my own artist, I have my own vision.

00:01:42.764 --> 00:01:46.347
♫ Can't read my poker face

00:01:47.924 --> 00:01:51.194
I suppose that there's one interpretation

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:54.906
of the female to sing a
love song and look pretty

00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:58.421
and just, you know, I
guess do what everyone

00:01:58.421 --> 00:02:00.254
thinks you should do.

00:02:00.254 --> 00:02:01.925
But it's just not what inspires me,

00:02:01.925 --> 00:02:05.175
and I'm inspired not just by what I see

00:02:06.484 --> 00:02:10.193
in terms of the future of
music, or tech, or fashion,

00:02:10.193 --> 00:02:13.339
I see also my fans, and I think about

00:02:13.339 --> 00:02:16.298
what they want to
experience, and they love

00:02:16.298 --> 00:02:20.722
to feel out of touch with
reality for a moment.

00:02:20.722 --> 00:02:23.062
So it might feel shocking, maybe,

00:02:23.062 --> 00:02:25.094
but I really think it's just this sort of

00:02:25.094 --> 00:02:29.261
surreal fantasy that I take
pleasure in creating for you.

00:02:30.444 --> 00:02:33.909
Someone asked once what
they, or many times, I think

00:02:33.909 --> 00:02:35.681
what I thought it meant to be an artist,

00:02:35.681 --> 00:02:39.650
and I said, what I think an
artist needs, essentially,

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.817
more than discipline, or even
talent, is a perspective.

00:02:45.502 --> 00:02:49.646
So if you are always changing what you do,

00:02:49.646 --> 00:02:51.571
based on what other people say,

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:53.648
you completely lose
yourself, and you become

00:02:53.648 --> 00:02:56.790
just, you know, something
that's constantly affected

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.148
by what's around you, as
opposed to stable and your own,

00:03:00.148 --> 00:03:02.815
and autonomous in your identity.

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:07.719
I've been making music now
with, you know, Tony Bennett

00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:11.641
for a couple of years, and
Elton John is, you know,

00:03:11.641 --> 00:03:15.808
one of my closest friends,
and his husband, David.

00:03:17.781 --> 00:03:21.948
They just have showed me that
I'm a baby in my artistry.

00:03:23.575 --> 00:03:26.408
And I just have to keep
making music and keep going.

00:03:26.408 --> 00:03:28.899
So my focus, really, for this
album is showing that, to

00:03:28.899 --> 00:03:32.770
to young girls, and to boys,
that want to make music,

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:36.080
that you don't have to
get lucky on the internet,

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.499
it's not a social media
game, you don't have to find

00:03:40.499 --> 00:03:44.431
the hot producer, and get
the hot track to be a star.

00:03:44.431 --> 00:03:47.653
You can just learn how
to play an instrument

00:03:47.653 --> 00:03:50.270
and sit in your room, and practice,

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.983
and write songs and
poetry, and then you can go

00:03:52.983 --> 00:03:55.530
around the corner to the
bar and meet other artists

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.277
that do the same thing that you do,

00:03:57.277 --> 00:03:59.975
and you can hang out and talk
about music and write songs,

00:03:59.975 --> 00:04:02.391
and then you can play at
the spot around the corner,

00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:06.558
gain one fan, and then do it
over and over and over again

00:04:07.511 --> 00:04:08.584
until you're discovered.

00:04:08.584 --> 00:04:12.693
For me, you know, I did say in
that skit that the other way

00:04:12.693 --> 00:04:15.677
to earn applause is to earn it.

00:04:15.677 --> 00:04:17.107
- Hard work.
- Hard work.

00:04:17.107 --> 00:04:19.835
And discipline, and rehearsal.

00:04:19.835 --> 00:04:22.979
That's always the most
thrilling way to earn applause.

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:25.137
And everything that you've seen me do

00:04:25.137 --> 00:04:28.103
I've always rehearsed a lot for it.

00:04:28.103 --> 00:04:32.298
So, there's a lot of
passion that goes behind it.

00:04:32.298 --> 00:04:33.832
- [Adam] When se was 17 years old,

00:04:33.832 --> 00:04:35.657
Stefani got her high school diploma

00:04:35.657 --> 00:04:37.347
and left the nuns to join the prestigious

00:04:37.347 --> 00:04:39.068
Tisch School of Arts,

00:04:39.068 --> 00:04:41.923
A school entirely dedicated
to artistic disciplines.

00:04:41.923 --> 00:04:43.544
She took numerous classes, such as

00:04:43.544 --> 00:04:45.972
Art Story, Music, Theater, and Dancing.

00:04:45.972 --> 00:04:48.082
- I was really blessed
and lucky to get in.

00:04:48.082 --> 00:04:50.582
I got in very early, I was 17,

00:04:52.606 --> 00:04:55.448
and I was one of 20 people

00:04:55.448 --> 00:04:58.948
in America that got into the school early.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.939
And I was really happy and excited,

00:05:02.939 --> 00:05:05.285
and I just felt like all
my dreams were coming true.

00:05:05.285 --> 00:05:07.301
And it was a wonderful school, but,

00:05:07.301 --> 00:05:09.384
it was very factory-like,

00:05:10.869 --> 00:05:13.182
and not in the Warhol factory-like way,

00:05:13.182 --> 00:05:15.763
more like a sausage link factory,

00:05:15.763 --> 00:05:18.735
where everyone was taught
the same sorts of things,

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:22.298
and I think what I didn't
like about the school

00:05:22.298 --> 00:05:25.692
was I felt that they wanted
us all to be the same

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:28.442
and possess the same skills, and,

00:05:29.531 --> 00:05:33.107
I had something very
different inside of me.

00:05:33.107 --> 00:05:34.844
- [Adam] Her days at
the Tisch School of Arts

00:05:34.844 --> 00:05:37.391
were very busy, her evenings too.

00:05:37.391 --> 00:05:42.047
Stefani made her debut as
a singer in New York bars.

00:05:42.047 --> 00:05:45.130
♫ Holding on to love

00:05:46.105 --> 00:05:47.459
Every night she would test out

00:05:47.459 --> 00:05:49.401
and adapt her own compositions.

00:05:49.401 --> 00:05:50.997
This experience enabled her to find

00:05:50.997 --> 00:05:53.289
the secret to writing good songs.

00:05:53.289 --> 00:05:54.936
Stefani made one of the most important

00:05:54.936 --> 00:05:56.302
decisions of her life.

00:05:56.302 --> 00:05:59.524
She decided to quit school,
and dedicate herself to music.

00:05:59.524 --> 00:06:02.581
- What did they say, your
parents, when you left?

00:06:02.581 --> 00:06:06.460
- My parents, well my mother
cried, and my father said,

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:10.350
"You have one year, and you
have to support yourself."

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:11.933
And I said, "Okay."

00:06:13.223 --> 00:06:15.989
For me, I was never interested

00:06:15.989 --> 00:06:17.448
in anyone taking care of me.

00:06:17.448 --> 00:06:20.424
I even had a job when I was 15 years old,

00:06:20.424 --> 00:06:22.104
because I liked to be independent,

00:06:22.104 --> 00:06:23.678
I liked to do things on my own terms.

00:06:23.678 --> 00:06:26.925
So, I left school and I
got three different jobs,

00:06:26.925 --> 00:06:29.178
and my dad paid my rent for three months,

00:06:29.178 --> 00:06:31.446
and then, when the three months was up,

00:06:31.446 --> 00:06:33.559
I had to pay, and it was great.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.058
It was wonderfully liberating.

00:06:36.058 --> 00:06:38.985
- Is it difficult now, with the pressure,

00:06:38.985 --> 00:06:42.880
I would feel, or think,
that there would be pressure

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.866
to continue to outdo
yourself, or to continue

00:06:45.866 --> 00:06:48.317
to express yourself.

00:06:48.317 --> 00:06:50.419
- There's always that pressure, but,

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:54.586
the best thing about it,
is it's only a door away.

00:06:55.468 --> 00:06:59.653
It's like you, you know, I've
got the first album door,

00:06:59.653 --> 00:07:03.060
it was open, it's great, door stays open,

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.790
I see it like a hallway (laughing)

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:07.926
That sounds, do I sound crazy?

00:07:07.926 --> 00:07:08.951
- No you do not.

00:07:08.951 --> 00:07:11.384
- It's a hallway with wooden doors.

00:07:11.384 --> 00:07:14.065
The first door's open, that's that album,

00:07:14.065 --> 00:07:16.030
you walk down, there's another door,

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.004
that's the next album, and then,

00:07:18.004 --> 00:07:21.762
there's one more door,
and it's coming up, right?

00:07:21.762 --> 00:07:26.726
And I'm banging on the walls,
and I'm looking for hammers,

00:07:26.726 --> 00:07:29.208
I'm trying to figure out
where the machine guns are,

00:07:29.208 --> 00:07:32.541
I just want to get to that frickin door.

00:07:33.565 --> 00:07:35.875
- Door, right. But you know it's there.

00:07:35.875 --> 00:07:39.837
- I know it's there, but can I open it?

00:07:39.837 --> 00:07:42.819
It's not the same as just writing a song.

00:07:42.819 --> 00:07:44.652
It's writing the next,

00:07:47.768 --> 00:07:49.819
it's writing the next chapter

00:07:49.819 --> 00:07:53.206
of whatever lobe is in my brain

00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:55.835
that's waiting to be unlocked, and

00:07:55.835 --> 00:07:58.002
if people think that I sound narcissistic

00:07:58.002 --> 00:08:01.759
it's fine, because you know,
Oprah, it's all I've got,

00:08:01.759 --> 00:08:03.714
is my creativity.

00:08:03.714 --> 00:08:06.239
So I walk down that hallway and,

00:08:06.239 --> 00:08:08.443
it happened recently.

00:08:08.443 --> 00:08:09.893
- The door opened.

00:08:09.893 --> 00:08:13.109
- The door opened. (laughing)

00:08:13.109 --> 00:08:16.116
It opened, it blast
opened, and once it opens

00:08:16.116 --> 00:08:19.521
the light shoots right through and I'm in.

00:08:19.521 --> 00:08:21.954
And then I can relax, because I know

00:08:21.954 --> 00:08:23.485
I broke through the door.

00:08:23.485 --> 00:08:25.908
- So then you can, is
that when the words flow,

00:08:25.908 --> 00:08:27.908
the lyrics, the harmony,

00:08:32.385 --> 00:08:33.931
- It's the journey.

00:08:33.931 --> 00:08:36.781
- Ah, got it.
- It's the journey.

00:08:36.781 --> 00:08:39.614
It's the new journey of all of us.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.400
- And is there something that you can do

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:47.337
to help the creative process,
the doors, open more readily?

00:08:47.337 --> 00:08:49.315
Is there something you can do?

00:08:49.315 --> 00:08:51.047
- I have a couple things that I do.

00:08:51.047 --> 00:08:55.507
The newest thing that I do,
I don't read a damn thing.

00:08:55.507 --> 00:08:57.507
No press, no television,

00:08:59.861 --> 00:09:02.581
no if my mom calls and says,

00:09:02.581 --> 00:09:04.498
"Did you hear about..."

00:09:05.599 --> 00:09:08.562
I don't want to know
nothing, about anything,

00:09:08.562 --> 00:09:11.371
that is going on in relation to music.

00:09:11.371 --> 00:09:13.398
Obviously I want to know about the world.

00:09:13.398 --> 00:09:17.565
I shut it all off, and other
than this interview, Oprah,

00:09:19.045 --> 00:09:22.294
I do not intend to speak to
anyone for a very long time.

00:09:22.294 --> 00:09:24.461
This is my moment, for me,

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:29.070
to experience a rebirth with my own music.

00:09:30.249 --> 00:09:33.332
I have to shut it all out, the noise,

00:09:34.846 --> 00:09:36.525
you know the noise.

00:09:36.525 --> 00:09:41.364
The noise, it's loud, you
have to cancel out the noise.

00:09:41.364 --> 00:09:44.532
And remember that it's
your thoughts that matter.

00:09:44.532 --> 00:09:46.582
- You're so smart.

00:09:46.582 --> 00:09:48.042
You are very wise.

00:09:48.042 --> 00:09:49.172
- Well, you are wise.

00:09:49.172 --> 00:09:50.782
- No, that's how you do
it. That is how you do it.

00:09:50.782 --> 00:09:52.115
- You have to...

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:53.864
- Okay.

00:09:53.864 --> 00:09:56.878
- I just learned how
much more wise I am now,

00:09:56.878 --> 00:10:01.045
and how much I've learned,
and how important family is

00:10:02.767 --> 00:10:06.850
and friendship, and how
your career and making it

00:10:07.851 --> 00:10:10.369
is important, but it's
not more important than

00:10:10.369 --> 00:10:13.281
keeping plans with your
grandma and your dad, you know,

00:10:13.281 --> 00:10:16.281
and making sure that you are on time

00:10:19.003 --> 00:10:22.170
for your mom's event that she's doing.

00:10:23.663 --> 00:10:25.324
Whatever it is that you're working on,

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:30.280
that's what I've really learned
going into my, you know,

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:33.428
leaving my twenties, is
that it was time to have

00:10:33.428 --> 00:10:35.019
more balance in my life.

00:10:35.019 --> 00:10:37.519
How do I keep my ego in check?

00:10:39.073 --> 00:10:43.240
I am always conscious of
empowering people around me.

00:10:44.801 --> 00:10:49.124
I want everyone in my team
to know that they all play

00:10:49.124 --> 00:10:51.607
the same, equal part that I play.

00:10:51.607 --> 00:10:54.804
And making everything happen,
that happens every day.

00:10:54.804 --> 00:10:56.569
This is my family.

00:10:56.569 --> 00:10:58.569
They are my inspiration.

00:11:00.364 --> 00:11:01.864
I'm a loyal bitch.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:05.893
- Joanne!

00:11:08.832 --> 00:11:10.696
(clapping and cheering)

00:11:10.696 --> 00:11:14.185
I make it a very strong
effort, every second,

00:11:14.185 --> 00:11:17.275
to be the nicest person that I can be.

00:11:17.275 --> 00:11:19.472
- So let's look at some
of your headline-making

00:11:19.472 --> 00:11:21.180
outrageous outfits that you designed.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.097
Tell us about this one.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.556
- Oh yeah, well that's Alexander McQueen,

00:11:27.556 --> 00:11:30.151
and I added the hat to
it, it's an archive,

00:11:30.151 --> 00:11:32.585
it's the oldest archive
in the McQueen collection,

00:11:32.585 --> 00:11:34.354
and it was meant to be a continuation

00:11:34.354 --> 00:11:38.232
of the VMA performance,
so after, I, the princess,

00:11:38.232 --> 00:11:41.056
had been murdered by the paparazzi,

00:11:41.056 --> 00:11:43.414
the red lace was meant to symbolize,

00:11:43.414 --> 00:11:45.533
sort of, my eternal martyrdom.

00:11:45.533 --> 00:11:46.611
- And this?

00:11:46.611 --> 00:11:48.551
- Oh, well that's, you know,

00:11:48.551 --> 00:11:51.798
that's Jean de Castelbajac outfit,

00:11:51.798 --> 00:11:54.520
and I don't like to wear fur, so it was

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.825
my way of making a comment about fur.

00:11:56.825 --> 00:12:00.022
Because it looks like a
bunch of dead muppets.

00:12:00.022 --> 00:12:01.698
- Like a bunch of dead muppets.

00:12:01.698 --> 00:12:03.115
- Yeah.
- Absolutely.

00:12:03.115 --> 00:12:05.357
- Oh, that's the queen.

00:12:05.357 --> 00:12:06.931
Well, I kind of wanted

00:12:06.931 --> 00:12:09.482
to do an homage to the
queen, so that was my

00:12:09.482 --> 00:12:13.700
Queen Elizabeth I outfit,
but it's made out of latex,

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:15.278
so it's still Gaga.

00:12:15.278 --> 00:12:16.836
- Still Gaga.

00:12:16.836 --> 00:12:18.706
So, do you spend a lot of time

00:12:18.706 --> 00:12:21.051
thinking about it, or does it just come?

00:12:21.051 --> 00:12:23.900
The outfits, the creative expression?

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:26.289
- The inspiration comes naturally, but,

00:12:26.289 --> 00:12:31.084
I put a lot of time and
effort into everything I do,

00:12:31.084 --> 00:12:33.082
to the point that I do nothing else.

00:12:33.082 --> 00:12:35.358
I wake up in the morning and
I work creatively all day,

00:12:35.358 --> 00:12:37.664
and all future things that I'm doing

00:12:37.664 --> 00:12:38.888
- So what's a day like?

00:12:38.888 --> 00:12:40.516
You're thinking about--

00:12:40.516 --> 00:12:42.989
- I wake up in the morning
and I get on my computer,

00:12:42.989 --> 00:12:44.713
and I have files, lists of references,

00:12:44.713 --> 00:12:46.422
and I have reference boards with

00:12:46.422 --> 00:12:48.152
all of my materials on them,

00:12:48.152 --> 00:12:50.708
and I basically call the
House of Gaga all day,

00:12:50.708 --> 00:12:53.113
and argue with them
about what I want to do,

00:12:53.113 --> 00:12:54.882
while they tell me, "You can't do that."

00:12:54.882 --> 00:12:56.652
And I say, "Yes I can bleed to death

00:12:56.652 --> 00:13:00.495
on domestic television, if I want to."

00:13:00.495 --> 00:13:04.873
For me, it's all about being
provocative is not just

00:13:04.873 --> 00:13:07.133
about getting people's attention,

00:13:07.133 --> 00:13:09.416
it's about releasing
something that affects people

00:13:09.416 --> 00:13:12.051
in a real way, and in a positive way.

00:13:12.051 --> 00:13:15.344
So, all the things that I
do, in terms of the fame,

00:13:15.344 --> 00:13:17.106
and in terms of the fame monster,

00:13:17.106 --> 00:13:19.489
it's meant to sort of make
it a bit easier to swallow,

00:13:19.489 --> 00:13:23.656
this, kind of, horrific
media world that we live in.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:27.708
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:27.708 --> 00:13:30.566
I made this video because
Virginia Castillo asked me to.

00:13:30.566 --> 00:13:32.130
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:33.630
